>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `linarith_solver`

``` hol4
linarithLib.linarith_solver : Traverse.ssolver
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Provides the named unsafe simplifier solver `"lin_arith"`.

The solver proves arithmetic side conditions from the simplifier's
`context_thms` and persistent `[arith]` facts. It uses direct forward
arithmetic, without the operator splitting and Boolean preprocessing of
`LINARITH_TAC`. Arithmetic propositions share the cache of
`LINARITH_ss`, so `clear_linarith_caches` applies to them; a
proposition outside linear arithmetic is not cached and is proved only
when the simplifier's own context is itself arithmetically
contradictory. A context with no arithmetic in it is declined at once,
whatever `[arith]` facts have been declared: those facts hold together,
so they refute nothing without an arithmetic context assumption to
refute it with. The solver is
already installed in the clasimp and Aesop
simpsets. It can be added to another simpset with
`simpLib.add_unsafe_solver`, suppressed for one invocation with
`Excl "lin_arith"`, or removed from a simpset with
`simpLib.remove_solver "lin_arith"`.

Here *unsafe* determines when the simplifier tries the solver. Every
successful result remains a kernel-checked theorem.

### Failure

The solver raises a `HOL_ERR` when its requested proposition does not
follow by supported direct linear arithmetic. The simplifier treats this
as the solver being inapplicable and may try another solver.

### Example

``` hol4
val arithmetic_ss =
  simpLib.add_unsafe_solver linarithLib.linarith_solver
    (bossLib.srw_ss ());

Theorem linarith_solver_example:
  !x y z : num. x <= y /\ y <= z ==> x <= z
Proof
  rpt strip_tac >>
  simpLib.FULL_SIMP_TAC arithmetic_ss []
QED
```

